WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.780
Some of the characters who've popped up in
Disney+'s Mandalorian are simply callbacks

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:06.300
to early Star Wars movies.

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:10.800
Some, on the other hand, answer decades-old
questions — while others just bring up more

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.180
questions than the show may even have time
to answer.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.880
Pedro Pascal's Mandalorian — or "Mando"
as head of the Bounty Hunters' Guild Greef

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:22.500
Karga calls him — is a walking collection
of mysteries and callbacks to older Star Wars

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:23.000
lore.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.660
There's his rifle, which we see him using
early against the beast that tries to drag

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:32.290
his ship underwater in Chapter 1 and later
to disintegrate Jawas and rival bounty hunters.

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:36.550
It's the same weapon Boba Fett is armed with
during his animated introduction in the Star

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:40.380
Wars Holiday Special, though we never see
Fett armed with it in the films.

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:44.360
We don't know a lot about the Mandalorian
people yet, save for what we've gathered from

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:48.930
scenes with the mysterious Armorer and other
Mandalorian survivors — but the very presence

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:53.200
of Mando — foundling or not — tempts us
to hope we'll get to see more of this fictional

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.360
culture, and maybe some answers to some more
basic questions.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:59.200
For instance, will we ever see Mando without
his helmet?

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:00.820
"So what happens if you take that thing off?

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:03.640
They come after you and kill you?"

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:05.740
"No.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:08.640
You just can't ever put it back on again."

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:13.060
Plot-wise, the goggled, long-snouted character
who calls the Mandalorian a ride early in

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:15.060
Chapter 1 doesn't do all that much.

00:01:15.229 --> 00:01:19.800
He calls Mando a droid-driven speeder, Mando
passes on the droid, and the alien calls him

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:25.600
another speeder that's literally falling apart
and driven by famous funny nerd Brian Posehn.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:30.490
But the goggled flutist has more meaning not
only as far as Star Wars lore is concerned,

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:33.460
but the ancient narrative roots The Mandalorian
is tapping into.

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:38.180
More involved Star Wars fans will recognize
the flutist as an alien called a Kubaz — it's

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:42.820
a Kubaz in A New Hope who stalks the heroes
around Mos Eisley and turns them in to the

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:44.080
Imperial Storm Troopers.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:45.700
But he's more than just an Easter egg.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:50.799
When discussing the advice George Lucas gave
him, Mandalorian creator Jon Favreau revealed

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:54.520
that Lucas reminded him to think about ancient
stories of heroism.

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:58.960
Lucas was heavily influenced by the writing
of Joseph Campbell, whose book The Hero with

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.460
a Thousand Faces breaks down the commonalities
among heroic tales in different cultures.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:08.440
In light of this, the flutist Kubaz seems
like a clear reflection of Lucas' advice,

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:11.080
as the scene is reminiscent of the myth of
the River Styx.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:16.030
In ancient Greek myth, Styx was a river crossed
by the newly dead by way of the ferryman Charon,

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:19.021
but the passage cost a coin — like the one
Mando tosses to the Kubaz.

00:02:19.021 --> 00:02:24.580
A Kubaz sniper later appears in Chapter 4,
attempting to assassinate Baby Yoda, which

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:28.890
— regardless as to whether this is the same
Kubaz flutist or not — further reinforces

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:31.100
the whole "Kubaz as an agent of death" idea.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:33.280
That's some pretty literary stuff, Favreau!

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:37.740
There are a couple of interesting things going
on with "The Client," the cold Imperial who

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:42.110
hires the Mandalorian to bring him "the asset,"
and who reacts with such a brimming undertone

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:46.420
of threat when Mando dares to ask him what
will happen to the asset once he has it.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:50.740
For starters, there's the surprising but perfect
casting choice of Werner Herzog.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:55.780
While Herzog has acted before, he's best known
as an acclaimed director of existential art

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:57.680
films and think-y documentaries.

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:02.120
Even setting aside the casting choice, the
Client tells us a lot about the post-Imperial

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:06.220
Star Wars world while at the same time leading
to some interesting questions.

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:10.420
The Client is presumably someone who wielded
a good deal of power in the Empire before

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:10.920
its fall.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:15.840
In spite of the Empire's defeat in Return
of the Jedi, the Client has significant resources

00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:19.510
and influence — at least enough to operate
without too much concern.

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:24.160
He's managed to keep a loyal band of stormtroopers
by his side, as well as some kind of intelligence

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:24.660
network.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:27.200
Clearly, the Empire is not as dead as the
Death Star.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:32.580
If Kuiil — the alien farmer in The Mandalorian's
first two chapters who helps Mando find the

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:36.700
asset and get back home — looks familiar
to you, there are a few reasons why that might

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:37.360
be the case.

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:41.760
Sure, it could be because you recognize the
facial similarities to Nick Nolte, who voices

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:46.010
the elderly farmer — but it might also be
that you remember one of the diminutive races

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:48.750
we meet in 1980's The Empire Strikes Back.

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:52.540
Kuiil is an Ugnaught, a race seen in Bespin's
floating Cloud City.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:56.580
They're the guys working the machines when
Han Solo is frozen in carbonite, and they're

00:03:56.580 --> 00:04:00.980
the ones playing catch with C-3PO's golden
head when Chewbacca saves it and the rest

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:02.260
of the droid from being scrapped.

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:07.000
When the Mandalorian offers Kuiil a place
aboard his ship at the end of Chapter 2, the

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:11.070
farmer tells the bounty hunter he is honored,
but that he long worked to free himself from

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:12.320
the labor of others.

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:15.450
Could it be that Kuiil was one of the workers
we see in Cloud City?

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:16.300
It's possible.

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:17.300
We have spoken.

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:18.660
Baby Yoda.

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:19.680
Can you believe it?

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:20.760
It's too frickin' cute.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:22.280
"Stop touching things."

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:34.760
Chapter 1 of The Mandalorian ends with the
huge reveal that the asset — Mando's target

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:38.590
and apparently the target of every single
member of the Bounty Hunter's Guild — is

00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:42.380
an infant member of whatever species the late
Jedi Master Yoda hails from.

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:43.640
We're not being lazy here.

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:48.350
No one knows much about Yoda's species and
George Lucas kept it unnamed on purpose.

00:04:48.350 --> 00:04:52.470
But the baby's presence and Mando's affection
for it has some huge implications for the

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:53.520
Star Wars mythos.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:58.040
For one thing, the way the infant saves Mando
from the horned beast in Chapter 2 tells us

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:01.920
Yoda's species is even more attuned with the
Force than we ever imagined.

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:06.600
Its age leads us to a lot of questions — at
50 years old, Baby Yoda would have been alive

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:08.510
during the era of the prequel films.

00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:11.660
Could it be that it was hidden just before
the Empire came to power?

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:13.100
Did Yoda know about the baby?

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:16.360
Not to mention, who was guarding Baby Yoda
in the first place?

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:21.180
Who was paying for all the men that the Mandalorian
and IG-11 had to kill to get to it?

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:23.480
And, more importantly, why were they guarding
it?

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:27.840
In Chapter 4 we meet, a former shock trooper
for the Rebellion.

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:32.540
Mando takes note of Cara as soon as he arrives
on Sorgan, worrying she's there to kill him

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:36.960
and take Baby Yoda to whatever horrific fate
the last remnants of the Empire have in store

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:37.460
for him.

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:42.800
Cara notices Mando too, makes similar assumptions
about him, and the two mercenaries enjoy a

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:46.740
fairly brutal melee before making peace — and
throwing back some soup together.

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:55.700
"Want some soup?"

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:01.160
After their fight, Cara tells Mando she became
a mercenary after the fall of the Empire.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:05.500
She describes being relegated to duties she
couldn't stomach like protecting delegates

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:09.940
and suppressing riots once her role of shock
trooper was over, at which point she became

00:06:09.940 --> 00:06:10.780
a gun for hire.

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:15.150
We don't know a lot of details about Cara's
character yet, but what little we know — and

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:16.860
what we see from her — tells us a lot.

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:20.000
More than anything, we know Cara has a strong
moral compass.

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:24.420
Cara's the first mercenary character we meet
who we know for sure harbors loyalties to

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:25.020
the Rebellion.

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:30.300
As much as she expresses doubts, she refuses
to abandon the farming village and even saves

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:31.320
Baby Yoda's life.

00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:35.600
The fact that she assumed Mando "had a fob"
on her means she's on the outs with the Guild.

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:40.720
Perhaps, like Mando, Cara chose conscience
over duty, and that's what has her in hiding.

